# STACKIT Landing Zone: **${platform_identifier}**

%{ if playground_mode ~}
> **Playground mode.** The platform identifier carries a random suffix so this deployment does not
> occupy a name for good, and nothing here is protected against deletion. Do not publish this
> platform or the building block definitions it registered to other workspaces. Redeploy with
> `playground_mode` set to false for a platform that is actually used.

%{ endif ~}
## Details

| Property | Value |
|----------|-------|
| **Organization** | [Open in STACKIT Portal](${organization_url}) (`${organization_id}`) |
%{ if platform_contract_mode ~}
| **Backplane Service Account** | `${service_account_email}` |
%{ for key, lz in contract_landing_zones ~}
| **Landing Zone `${lz.name}`** | ${lz.display_name}, folder `${lz.parent_container_id}`%{ if lz.network_area_id != null }, network area `${lz.network_area_id}`%{ endif } |
%{ endfor ~}

This platform runs on the platform contract of a STACKIT Landing Zone Accelerator: it created no
STACKIT resources, and every definition it registered acts as `${service_account_email}`.

### Set up the service account in the STACKIT portal

Until these steps are done, every run of a definition below fails to authenticate.

1. Open the service account `${service_account_email}` in the STACKIT portal.
2. Under **Federated identity providers**, add one provider per definition, each with:
   - **Issuer**: `${workload_identity_federation.issuer}`
   - **Assertions**: `aud` equals `${workload_identity_federation.audience}`, and `sub` equals the
     subject below.

   | Definition | Subject (`sub equals`) |
   |------------|------------------------|
%{ for key, subject in workload_identity_federation.subjects ~}
   | `${key}` | `${subject}` |
%{ endfor ~}

3. Grant the service account these roles on the organization `${organization_id}`:
   - `resource-manager.admin`, to create the tenant projects in the target folders,
   - `iam.member-admin`, to grant project roles and add project users to the organization,
   - `iam.service-account-admin`, to create service accounts in tenant projects.

The building block output `workload_identity_federation` holds the same issuer, audience and
subjects, to set this up as code. A subject changes when a definition moves to another runner.
%{ else ~}
%{ if root_folder_name != "" ~}
| **Root Folder** | [Open in STACKIT Portal](${root_folder_url}) (`${root_folder_name}`) |
%{ endif ~}
| **Landing Zone Folder** | [Open in STACKIT Portal](${lz_folder_url}) (`${lz_folder_container_id}`) |
| **Foundation Project** | [Open in STACKIT Portal](${foundation_project_url}) (`${foundation_project_id}`) |
| **Foundation Project Service Account** | [Open in STACKIT Portal](${service_account_url}) (`${service_account_email}`) |
%{ if network_enabled ~}
| **Hub Network Area** | [Open in STACKIT Portal](${network_area_url}) (`${network_area_id}`) |
| **Hub Network Area Building Block** | @buildingblock[${network_area_hub_uuid}] |
| **Networked Landing Zone** | `${networked_landingzone_name}` |
%{ endif ~}
%{ endif ~}

%{ if platform_contract_mode ~}
Application teams can now request STACKIT projects against the landing zones above.
%{ else ~}
%{ if network_enabled ~}
Hub-and-spoke networking is enabled. Application teams can order the **STACKIT Network** building
block inside their own projects in the `${networked_landingzone_name}` landing zone to get a routed
spoke network drawn from the hub's address plan.
%{ else ~}
Application teams can now request STACKIT projects against the `${platform_identifier}-default`
landing zone. Provide a `network` object to additionally enable hub-and-spoke networking.
%{ endif ~}
%{ endif ~}

> [!TIP]
> ## Build a Kubernetes platform on top
>
> The [STACKIT Kubernetes Platform](https://hub.meshcloud.io/reference-architectures/stackit-kubernetes)
> reference architecture runs a full SKE developer platform on top of this landing zone. It takes one
> value from this landing zone. Paste this block into its **STACKIT Landing Zone** input:
>
%{ if platform_contract_mode ~}
> ```hcl
> {
>   service_account_federation_bbd_version_ref = { uuid = "${service_account_federation_bbd_version_uuid}" }
> }
> ```
>
> Set its **STACKIT Accelerator** input to an accelerator project and a service account owning it.
> The service account federation runs as `${service_account_email}`, which needs `editor` on that
> project.
%{ else ~}
> ```hcl
> {
>   platform_ref = { uuid = "${platform_ref.uuid}" }
>   landingzone_refs = {
%{ for key, ref in landingzone_refs ~}
>     ${key} = { name = "${ref.name}" }
%{ endfor ~}
>   }
>   service_account_bbd_version_ref            = { uuid = "${service_account_bbd_version_uuid}" }
>   service_account_federation_bbd_version_ref = { uuid = "${service_account_federation_bbd_version_uuid}" }
> }
> ```
%{ if network_enabled ~}
>
> Set its **Landing Zone Variant** to `networked` to run the cluster inside the hub-and-spoke network.
> Otherwise keep the default `default`, which puts the cluster in a **STACKIT Sandbox** project with
> STACKIT's default flat networking.
%{ endif ~}
%{ endif ~}
%{ if !identity_definitions_released ~}

> [!WARNING]
> The latest versions of the **STACKIT Service Account** and the **STACKIT Service Account
> Federation** definitions are drafts, and another workspace may only order released versions.
> Deploy this landing zone with `hub.bbd_draft = false`, publish both definitions to the
> marketplace, then run this building block again.
%{ endif ~}
